1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,361
2018. One spotlight on a television stage.

2
00:00:03,381 --> 00:00:07,130
Allison Ellsworth is pitching her
vinegar soda to five investors...

3
00:00:07,212 --> 00:00:09,260
and she is nine months pregnant.

4
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:11,978
In the one lit chair: Rohan Oza.

5
00:00:11,998 --> 00:00:14,790
He is about to offer her four
hundred thousand dollars.

6
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:16,680
Psst. Don't get too attached to me.

7
00:00:16,710 --> 00:00:17,915
We know what they don't.

8
00:00:17,942 --> 00:00:22,570
One day, a cola giant will pay one point
nine five billion dollars for this drink.

9
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:24,750
Two billion dollars? For vinegar?

10
00:00:24,770 --> 00:00:26,250
Same soda. Keep watching.

11
00:00:26,270 --> 00:00:27,440
Is she okay up there?

12
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:29,570
She's fine. I'm the one sweating.

13
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:32,340
Her son will be born ten
days after this pitch.

14
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:35,370
And that big number has a
part almost nobody noticed.

15
00:00:35,390 --> 00:00:39,110
So how did Poppi make one point
nine five billion dollars from soda?

16
00:00:39,130 --> 00:00:44,256
It starts around 2015, in a family
kitchen. Allison has stomach troubles.

17
00:00:44,276 --> 00:00:47,770
So she mixes apple cider
vinegar, fruit and soda water.

18
00:00:47,790 --> 00:00:51,717
Her husband, Stephen, helps sell
it at farmers' markets in Dallas.

19
00:00:51,737 --> 00:00:54,640
Within months, a Whole Foods
buyer agrees to stock it.

20
00:00:54,670 --> 00:00:56,305
Vinegar. In a soda.

21
00:00:56,325 --> 00:00:56,965
With fruit!

22
00:00:56,985 --> 00:00:58,805
Who drinks vinegar on purpose?

23
00:00:58,825 --> 00:00:59,935
People with stomachs?

24
00:00:59,955 --> 00:01:01,455
...Fine. You're in.

25
00:01:01,485 --> 00:01:05,607
The first eighteen months bring in about
five hundred thousand dollars of sales.

26
00:01:05,715 --> 00:01:07,375
Growing costs far more.

27
00:01:07,395 --> 00:01:10,975
Allison later told the Wall Street
Journal, as Fortune reported:

28
00:01:10,995 --> 00:01:15,145
"So we maxed out our credit cards,
sold one of our cars to buy bottles"...

29
00:01:15,165 --> 00:01:17,083
...and opened their own factory.

30
00:01:17,103 --> 00:01:20,575
Her husband, she said, told
her she was "absolutely crazy".

31
00:01:20,605 --> 00:01:23,165
Credit cards. A car. A factory.

32
00:01:23,185 --> 00:01:23,955
Is that a lot?

33
00:01:23,975 --> 00:01:25,805
For a family? That's everything.

34
00:01:25,825 --> 00:01:26,905
And if I don't sell?

35
00:01:26,925 --> 00:01:29,125
Then you're very expensive salad dressing.

36
00:01:29,155 --> 00:01:31,466
Then they practised one
pitch for six months...

37
00:01:31,486 --> 00:01:33,675
for a stage with five investors on it.

38
00:01:34,665 --> 00:01:37,615
Six months of practice.
Then the lights came on.

39
00:01:37,635 --> 00:01:41,887
The ask: four hundred thousand dollars
for ten percent of the company.

40
00:01:41,907 --> 00:01:44,405
That puts the whole thing at four million.

41
00:01:44,425 --> 00:01:48,555
Accounts of the episode say the
investors pushed hard: on how much more

42
00:01:48,575 --> 00:01:53,335
money it would need, on the valuation,
on the plan to run their own factory.

43
00:01:53,365 --> 00:01:54,635
No. No. No. No...

44
00:01:54,655 --> 00:01:55,515
That's four.

45
00:01:55,535 --> 00:01:56,885
...Anyone? Anyone at all?

46
00:01:56,915 --> 00:02:00,530
The fifth chair was Rohan Oza's.
He would pay the full ask...

47
00:02:00,550 --> 00:02:01,785
for twenty-five percent.

48
00:02:01,805 --> 00:02:04,480
Same money, two and a
half times the share.

49
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:06,883
By our arithmetic, his
offer valued the company

50
00:02:06,903 --> 00:02:09,725
at one point six million
dollars, not four.

51
00:02:09,755 --> 00:02:11,505
Four million, bro. Ten percent.

52
00:02:11,525 --> 00:02:13,255
One point six. A quarter.

53
00:02:13,275 --> 00:02:14,405
That's robbery!

54
00:02:14,425 --> 00:02:15,295
That's a deal.

55
00:02:15,315 --> 00:02:16,085
...I'll take it.

56
00:02:16,115 --> 00:02:20,925
She took it. She remembers thinking, "this
moment right here could change my life."

57
00:02:20,945 --> 00:02:23,195
Ten days after the
pitch, her son was born.

58
00:02:23,225 --> 00:02:24,924
Visiting hours are over.

59
00:02:24,944 --> 00:02:26,314
I'm family. Sort of.

60
00:02:26,334 --> 00:02:27,544
You're a beverage.

61
00:02:27,574 --> 00:02:30,934
The episode aired on Sunday
the ninth of December, 2018.

62
00:02:30,964 --> 00:02:33,034
Twenty-five percent of a vinegar company.

63
00:02:33,054 --> 00:02:33,894
Is that good?

64
00:02:33,914 --> 00:02:36,588
Depends what the other
seventy-five turns into.

65
00:02:36,608 --> 00:02:37,778
When do we find out?

66
00:02:37,798 --> 00:02:39,481
Ask me in six years.

67
00:02:39,511 --> 00:02:42,951
Years later, Oza recalled what
he had seen in that pitch.

68
00:02:42,971 --> 00:02:46,391
"Package, wrong. Brand, wrong.
Everything is wrong, basically.

69
00:02:46,411 --> 00:02:47,691
But this liquid, that's it."

70
00:02:47,721 --> 00:02:49,301
Label, wrong. Bottle, wrong.

71
00:02:49,321 --> 00:02:49,951
And me?

72
00:02:49,971 --> 00:02:50,951
You, I keep.

73
00:02:50,981 --> 00:02:53,961
He had bought the drink. Now
he wanted a new word on it.

74
00:02:54,951 --> 00:02:56,781
The first thing to go was the name.

75
00:02:56,801 --> 00:02:59,661
According to Mashed, "Mother"
could not be trademarked.

76
00:02:59,691 --> 00:03:01,151
I'd like to own my name, please.

77
00:03:01,171 --> 00:03:02,101
Not that one.

78
00:03:02,121 --> 00:03:03,261
...What about my face?

79
00:03:03,281 --> 00:03:03,861
Next.

80
00:03:03,891 --> 00:03:07,529
Later, Oza put it more politely:
"The name and package were

81
00:03:07,549 --> 00:03:10,481
not conducive of the big picture
that we're trying to solve."

82
00:03:10,501 --> 00:03:14,503
So in 2020 the bottle became
a can. The name became Poppi.

83
00:03:14,523 --> 00:03:16,811
The label said "prebiotic soda".

84
00:03:16,841 --> 00:03:18,391
Same soda. New word.

85
00:03:18,411 --> 00:03:19,901
You look... expensive.

86
00:03:19,921 --> 00:03:20,981
I feel expensive.

87
00:03:21,001 --> 00:03:22,061
You taste the same.

88
00:03:22,081 --> 00:03:23,121
Don't tell anyone.

89
00:03:23,151 --> 00:03:26,215
Oza's firm, CAVU Consumer
Partners, backed it.

90
00:03:26,235 --> 00:03:30,501
PepsiCo would later say CAVU funded
Poppi from its first seed round.

91
00:03:30,521 --> 00:03:35,141
The launch was set for March 2020...
at a trade show that Covid cancelled.

92
00:03:35,171 --> 00:03:36,181
Where is everybody?

93
00:03:36,201 --> 00:03:37,841
Home. Everybody's home.

94
00:03:37,861 --> 00:03:39,061
Then I'm going to their homes.

95
00:03:39,091 --> 00:03:43,110
So they sold online, through
Amazon and online grocers.

96
00:03:43,130 --> 00:03:48,101
By the end of 2020, one report says,
sales were about four million dollars.

97
00:03:48,121 --> 00:03:52,541
Then came one video. Food Republic
reports that a TikTok of Allison

98
00:03:52,658 --> 00:03:55,281
added a hundred thousand
in sales within a day.

99
00:03:55,311 --> 00:03:56,731
Bro. The can is viral.

100
00:03:56,751 --> 00:03:57,591
I'm a soda.

101
00:03:57,611 --> 00:03:59,310
Not anymore. Now you're content.

102
00:03:59,330 --> 00:04:00,110
Is that better?

103
00:04:00,130 --> 00:04:00,970
It's louder.

104
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:05,968
But sales are not cash. Allison told
Entrepreneur that at twenty million dollars of

105
00:04:05,988 --> 00:04:09,203
revenue, the company had
seventy-five thousand in the bank...

106
00:04:09,223 --> 00:04:11,180
and wasn't sure it could make payroll.

107
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:15,590
Stephen remembers "begging and pleading"
with suppliers for more time to pay.

108
00:04:15,620 --> 00:04:16,430
Pay Friday.

109
00:04:16,450 --> 00:04:17,780
How about... a Friday?

110
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:18,550
Which one?

111
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:20,960
They needed money that didn't
come from a credit card.

112
00:04:21,950 --> 00:04:24,850
The money came... with
famous faces attached.

113
00:04:24,870 --> 00:04:30,297
In August 2021, Poppi raised thirteen
million dollars, led by Oza's firm.

114
00:04:30,317 --> 00:04:32,155
Celebrities such as Halsey and Russell

115
00:04:32,175 --> 00:04:35,180
Westbrook joined in,
Dallas Innovates reported.

116
00:04:35,210 --> 00:04:36,560
Can in hand. Chin up.

117
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:38,100
Does anyone actually drink me?

118
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:39,710
Nobody sips, bro. They post.

119
00:04:39,740 --> 00:04:43,038
The cans sold for two
dollars forty-nine each,

120
00:04:43,058 --> 00:04:45,750
in more than seven thousand
five hundred stores.

121
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:47,770
Two forty-nine. For one can.

122
00:04:47,790 --> 00:04:49,310
You're not buying a soda.

123
00:04:49,330 --> 00:04:50,390
Then what am I buying?

124
00:04:50,410 --> 00:04:51,820
A picture of you holding it.

125
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:53,340
...It is a nice picture.

126
00:04:53,370 --> 00:04:57,160
In December 2022, another
twenty-five million dollars.

127
00:04:57,180 --> 00:05:00,380
Sales had grown a hundred and
forty-eight percent in a year.

128
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:05,319
By 2024, the company says, revenue was
about five hundred million dollars.

129
00:05:05,339 --> 00:05:08,820
More than a hundred times
2020, by our arithmetic.

130
00:05:08,850 --> 00:05:11,060
Four million to five hundred million!

131
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:12,800
Sales. Not profit.

132
00:05:12,820 --> 00:05:13,580
Same thing!

133
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:14,400
It is not.

134
00:05:14,430 --> 00:05:19,610
Then the biggest stage of all: the Super
Bowl, on the ninth of February 2025.

135
00:05:19,630 --> 00:05:21,150
You may have seen the ad.

136
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:23,250
What does a Super Bowl ad even cost?

137
00:05:23,270 --> 00:05:25,413
Doesn't matter. Everybody saw the can.

138
00:05:25,433 --> 00:05:27,103
I was looking at the snacks.

139
00:05:27,133 --> 00:05:31,933
Before its ad, Poppi sent branded vending
machines to thirty-two influencers.

140
00:05:31,953 --> 00:05:36,987
A rival brand, Olipop, posted that each
machine cost twenty-five thousand dollars.

141
00:05:37,007 --> 00:05:38,313
Keep that number in mind.

142
00:05:38,343 --> 00:05:39,763
Twenty-five grand for a fridge?

143
00:05:39,783 --> 00:05:41,073
Less! Way less!

144
00:05:41,093 --> 00:05:42,123
How much less?

145
00:05:42,143 --> 00:05:43,103
...Less.

146
00:05:43,133 --> 00:05:45,705
A Poppi spokesperson
called the figure "not

147
00:05:45,725 --> 00:05:48,697
only fabricated but
inflated by sixty percent".

148
00:05:48,717 --> 00:05:53,883
Allison posted a TikTok blaming
"misinformation" from "one of our competitors".

149
00:05:53,903 --> 00:05:56,970
A fight about a fridge. But
the fridge was not the point.

150
00:05:56,990 --> 00:06:00,313
And PepsiCo's own try was
about to be reported dead.

151
00:06:01,303 --> 00:06:03,333
Because PepsiCo had tried to build one.

152
00:06:03,353 --> 00:06:06,448
Its own prebiotic soda
was called Soulboost.

153
00:06:06,468 --> 00:06:11,843
In March 2025, days before the deal,
Bloomberg reported it had been scrapped.

154
00:06:11,873 --> 00:06:15,043
Fiber. Fizz. Fruit. Perfect.

155
00:06:15,063 --> 00:06:16,233
And the cool kids?

156
00:06:16,253 --> 00:06:17,163
They'll come.

157
00:06:17,183 --> 00:06:17,953
Build one?

158
00:06:17,973 --> 00:06:18,853
Tried that.

159
00:06:18,873 --> 00:06:19,693
Borrow one?

160
00:06:19,713 --> 00:06:21,393
Nobody lends you a can.

161
00:06:21,413 --> 00:06:23,043
Then... buy one.

162
00:06:23,073 --> 00:06:26,542
Coca-Cola had launched
its own, Simply Pop.

163
00:06:26,562 --> 00:06:30,507
And Olipop, the brand behind the
fridge post, was valued at one

164
00:06:30,527 --> 00:06:35,513
point eight five billion dollars in
February 2025, according to CNBC.

165
00:06:35,533 --> 00:06:39,587
Olipop's chief executive, Ben
Goodwin, had said in 2023 that

166
00:06:39,607 --> 00:06:43,513
both PepsiCo and Coca-Cola had
approached it about a sale.

167
00:06:43,543 --> 00:06:44,713
Will you be mine?

168
00:06:44,733 --> 00:06:46,123
Me? I'm vinegar.

169
00:06:46,143 --> 00:06:47,603
You're popular vinegar.

170
00:06:47,623 --> 00:06:48,383
How popular?

171
00:06:48,403 --> 00:06:50,003
Expensive popular.

172
00:06:50,033 --> 00:06:55,353
PepsiCo's chief executive, Ramon Laguarta,
would later say why it wanted one:

173
00:06:55,373 --> 00:06:59,233
consumers, he said, have a "growing
interest in health and wellness."

174
00:06:59,263 --> 00:07:01,073
Health... It used to say gut health?

175
00:07:01,093 --> 00:07:03,223
Shh. Not in front of the lawyers.

176
00:07:03,243 --> 00:07:04,183
What lawyers?

177
00:07:04,203 --> 00:07:05,093
...Those ones.

178
00:07:05,123 --> 00:07:06,946
Health was the word everyone wanted.

179
00:07:06,966 --> 00:07:09,723
But on Poppi's can, that
word was already in court.

180
00:07:10,713 --> 00:07:14,060
In May 2024, a shopper filed a class

181
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:16,459
action in federal court in California.

182
00:07:16,479 --> 00:07:19,643
Three more suits followed,
all before the same judge.

183
00:07:19,663 --> 00:07:23,590
The lawsuit said a can held about
two grams of prebiotic fiber...

184
00:07:23,610 --> 00:07:27,793
so you'd need more than four cans a
day for any effect, sugar included.

185
00:07:27,823 --> 00:07:29,183
Two grams of fiber?

186
00:07:29,203 --> 00:07:29,943
Allegedly.

187
00:07:29,963 --> 00:07:30,943
Four a day?

188
00:07:30,963 --> 00:07:31,723
Allegedly!

189
00:07:31,753 --> 00:07:35,342
Poppi denied all of it. It says
its label claims were true.

190
00:07:35,459 --> 00:07:39,973
It told Fortune a can has three grams
of fiber and five grams of sugar.

191
00:07:39,993 --> 00:07:43,078
In its own words, it had
also removed "any and all

192
00:07:43,098 --> 00:07:46,843
references to gut health"
from its labels in late 2023.

193
00:07:46,873 --> 00:07:48,393
Same soda. Fewer words.

194
00:07:48,413 --> 00:07:49,993
Funny. Tastes the same.

195
00:07:50,013 --> 00:07:51,083
It is the same.

196
00:07:51,103 --> 00:07:52,843
Then what were the words for?

197
00:07:52,873 --> 00:07:56,858
On the fourth of December
2024, a day-long mediation.

198
00:07:56,878 --> 00:08:00,983
On the sixteenth, the judge paused
the case: a deal in principle.

199
00:08:01,013 --> 00:08:01,773
Your side?

200
00:08:01,793 --> 00:08:03,303
Not guilty. But tired.

201
00:08:03,323 --> 00:08:04,143
Their side?

202
00:08:04,163 --> 00:08:05,133
Also tired.

203
00:08:05,163 --> 00:08:09,573
Three months later, Bloomberg would
report PepsiCo's Soulboost scrapped.

204
00:08:09,593 --> 00:08:13,953
The settlement: eight point nine million
dollars. Poppi admitted no fault.

205
00:08:13,973 --> 00:08:17,085
Buyers could claim about nine
dollars for a twelve-pack,

206
00:08:17,105 --> 00:08:19,223
and at least five dollars a household.

207
00:08:19,253 --> 00:08:20,348
Nine dollars.

208
00:08:20,368 --> 00:08:21,528
For all that soda?

209
00:08:21,548 --> 00:08:23,897
For a can that talked about your gut.

210
00:08:23,917 --> 00:08:24,747
Allegedly.

211
00:08:24,767 --> 00:08:27,307
...Next time, read the can. Every word.

212
00:08:27,337 --> 00:08:31,465
Now the timing. The settlement
was dated the fifth of March 2025.

213
00:08:31,485 --> 00:08:34,216
It was filed in court on
Friday the fourteenth.

214
00:08:34,236 --> 00:08:36,847
On Monday the seventeenth,
the buyer spoke.

215
00:08:36,877 --> 00:08:37,937
Friday to Monday?

216
00:08:37,957 --> 00:08:38,617
Tidy.

217
00:08:38,637 --> 00:08:39,527
Is that normal?

218
00:08:39,547 --> 00:08:40,407
It's tidy.

219
00:08:40,437 --> 00:08:44,567
The lawsuit was settled on paper. The
buyer could finally say its number.

220
00:08:45,557 --> 00:08:50,887
Monday, the seventeenth of March 2025.
PepsiCo announced it would buy Poppi.

221
00:08:50,917 --> 00:08:53,307
Sign here. And here. And here.

222
00:08:53,327 --> 00:08:54,187
What am I signing?

223
00:08:54,207 --> 00:08:55,167
Yourself.

224
00:08:55,197 --> 00:08:58,681
The price: one point nine
five billion dollars.

225
00:08:58,701 --> 00:09:01,467
By our arithmetic, that's
four thousand eight hundred

226
00:09:01,487 --> 00:09:04,407
and seventy-five times what
Oza offered on that stage.

227
00:09:04,437 --> 00:09:05,997
That's a lot of briefcases.

228
00:09:06,017 --> 00:09:07,707
Same soda. Bigger number.

229
00:09:07,727 --> 00:09:08,807
How many are there?

230
00:09:08,827 --> 00:09:10,327
Don't count. Just look up.

231
00:09:10,357 --> 00:09:13,963
But read the press release. That
figure includes three hundred million

232
00:09:13,983 --> 00:09:16,677
dollars of "anticipated
cash tax benefits".

233
00:09:16,697 --> 00:09:21,446
Of every hundred dollars in the headline,
about fifteen is that tax benefit.

234
00:09:21,466 --> 00:09:26,517
PepsiCo calls the rest, one point six
five billion, the net purchase price.

235
00:09:26,547 --> 00:09:27,987
So it's not two billion?

236
00:09:28,007 --> 00:09:29,117
The headline is.

237
00:09:29,137 --> 00:09:29,847
And the rest?

238
00:09:29,867 --> 00:09:31,157
The rest is a promise.

239
00:09:31,187 --> 00:09:33,848
On top, PepsiCo offered an earnout:

240
00:09:34,011 --> 00:09:38,557
more money later, if Poppi hit
targets. The release gave no amount.

241
00:09:38,577 --> 00:09:41,837
The deal closed on the
nineteenth of May 2025.

242
00:09:41,857 --> 00:09:45,400
How much went to the Ellsworths?
Nobody has published the split.

243
00:09:45,420 --> 00:09:49,577
Fortune reported more than a hundred
million dollars. No filing shows it.

244
00:09:49,607 --> 00:09:50,817
Over a hundred million?

245
00:09:50,837 --> 00:09:51,527
Reported.

246
00:09:51,547 --> 00:09:53,787
I can't pay a bill with "reported".

247
00:09:53,817 --> 00:09:57,676
Allison told Fortune that selling
had been the plan "from day one".

248
00:09:57,696 --> 00:10:02,247
And with PepsiCo, she said, "Now we have
a Ferrari underneath us for distribution."

249
00:10:02,277 --> 00:10:02,877
Faster!

250
00:10:02,897 --> 00:10:04,347
Hold on to your label.

251
00:10:04,367 --> 00:10:05,067
Which one?

252
00:10:05,097 --> 00:10:08,627
But one piece of that price was
never cash. It was a promise.

253
00:10:09,617 --> 00:10:11,897
Here is the part almost nobody noticed.

254
00:10:11,917 --> 00:10:14,946
According to Bloomberg, that
earnout could reach three hundred

255
00:10:14,966 --> 00:10:19,057
million dollars, for targets due
by the third quarter of 2027.

256
00:10:19,077 --> 00:10:24,480
In December 2025, PepsiCo valued it at
two hundred and seventy-eight million.

257
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:27,705
By June 2026, a hundred and seventeen.

258
00:10:27,725 --> 00:10:32,507
As of the fifth of September 2026,
a filing said about twelve million.

259
00:10:32,537 --> 00:10:33,827
Is that... my bonus?

260
00:10:33,847 --> 00:10:34,707
What's left of it.

261
00:10:34,727 --> 00:10:36,767
It could have been three hundred million!

262
00:10:36,787 --> 00:10:37,847
Could have.

263
00:10:37,877 --> 00:10:40,876
By our arithmetic, for each
hundred dollars PepsiCo

264
00:10:40,896 --> 00:10:44,200
once expected to pay, it
now expects about four.

265
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:47,617
Bloomberg reported the
cut in October 2026.

266
00:10:47,637 --> 00:10:51,048
Ken Shea of Bloomberg Intelligence
said that at this rate,

267
00:10:51,068 --> 00:10:55,016
it "seems plausible" PepsiCo
may not pay any of it.

268
00:10:55,036 --> 00:10:58,232
PepsiCo said Poppi grew
its sales volume by double

269
00:10:58,252 --> 00:11:01,047
digits in the third
quarter and gained share.

270
00:11:01,077 --> 00:11:02,227
Sales are up!

271
00:11:02,247 --> 00:11:03,937
Then why is the bonus down?

272
00:11:03,957 --> 00:11:05,247
Volume is up!

273
00:11:05,267 --> 00:11:06,966
That's not what I asked.

274
00:11:06,986 --> 00:11:08,126
...Next question.

275
00:11:08,156 --> 00:11:10,832
So where does this touch
you? In your fridge.

276
00:11:10,852 --> 00:11:15,158
The class action covered cans
bought from late January 2020,

277
00:11:15,240 --> 00:11:19,496
and its claim deadline closed on
the twenty-sixth of September 2025.

278
00:11:19,526 --> 00:11:21,066
This one just says soda.

279
00:11:21,086 --> 00:11:22,316
Read the small print.

280
00:11:22,336 --> 00:11:23,896
It says... soda.

281
00:11:23,926 --> 00:11:27,030
And next time you stand in
the soda aisle, read the can.

282
00:11:27,089 --> 00:11:28,996
Somebody paid a lot of
money for that word.

283
00:11:29,016 --> 00:11:30,560
The founders started again.

284
00:11:30,580 --> 00:11:35,489
On the ninth of November 2026, they
launched Jetty, a new drink at two

285
00:11:35,509 --> 00:11:39,356
forty-nine a bottle, with two and a
half million dollars of their own.

286
00:11:39,376 --> 00:11:43,326
Allison told Modern Retail: "No one
is excited to see me win again."

287
00:11:43,356 --> 00:11:45,086
New bottle. New word.

288
00:11:45,106 --> 00:11:46,126
Same founders.

289
00:11:46,156 --> 00:11:50,178
The next date to watch:
PepsiCo's next quarterly filing,

290
00:11:50,198 --> 00:11:52,756
when the value of that
bonus can change again.

291
00:11:52,786 --> 00:11:53,626
Same soda.

292
00:11:53,646 --> 00:11:54,636
Different word.

293
00:11:54,666 --> 00:11:58,771
So how did Poppi make one point
nine five billion dollars from soda?

294
00:11:58,791 --> 00:12:01,659
It changed the word on
the can, not the soda...

295
00:12:01,679 --> 00:12:03,646
and the number was never quite that big.
